exec(open('/home/claude/work/gita/gc.py').read())
V=[
X(1,'AN ANCIENT YOGA',
 S('The Sun god passing a glowing scroll to Manu','K','sun','VIVASVĀN → MANU'),
 'The Lord said: I taught this imperishable yoga to Vivasvān; Vivasvān told it to Manu, and Manu to Ikṣvāku.',
 [('imam vivasvate yogam','<b>this yoga to Vivasvān</b>'),('proktavān aham avyayam','<b>I taught, imperishable</b>'),('vivasvān manave prāha','<b>Vivasvān told Manu</b>'),('manuḥ ikṣvākave abravīt','<b>Manu told Ikṣvāku</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa reveals that the Gītā’s yoga is ancient. He taught it first to Vivasvān, the Sun; the Sun to Manu, father of humanity; Manu to Ikṣvāku, first king of the solar dynasty.',
 'Ikṣvāku’s line is the dynasty of Rāma. The teaching thus begins with kings, not hermits: it is a yoga for people in the world. Commentators note the word <i>avyaya</i>, imperishable: the yoga does not change, though it may be forgotten.',
 'Old wisdom is not out of date. The same truths are found again by each generation.',
 'The Gītā is <b>ancient wisdom, given again</b>.'),
X(2,'HANDED DOWN, THEN LOST',
 S('A chain of kings passing a light, the last link faded','','king','PARAMPARĀ'),
 '“Handed down thus from one to the next, the royal sages knew it. But over a long time, scorcher of foes, this yoga was lost to the world.”',
 [('evam paramparā-prāptam','<b>thus received in succession</b>'),('imam rāja-ṛṣayaḥ viduḥ','<b>the royal sages knew this</b>'),('saḥ kāleṇa iha mahatā','<b>by long time here</b>'),('yogaḥ naṣṭaḥ','<b>the yoga was lost</b>')],
 'The teaching passed down through a line of royal sages, then faded over time.',
 '<i>Paramparā</i>, an unbroken line of teachers, is how Indian traditions preserve knowledge. Commentators note that wisdom can be lost when it falls into the hands of the unworthy or simply through neglect.',
 'Wisdom needs living carriers. Pass on what you have learned.',
 'Wisdom passes down, <b>and can be lost</b>.'),
X(3,'BECAUSE YOU ARE MY FRIEND',
 S('Krishna placing a hand on Arjuna’s shoulder','KA','heart','BHAKTA · SAKHĀ'),
 '“That same ancient yoga I have told you today, because you are my devotee and my friend. This is the supreme secret.”',
 [('saḥ eva ayam mayā te adya','<b>this same one, by me, to you today</b>'),('yogaḥ proktaḥ purātanaḥ','<b>the ancient yoga is told</b>'),('bhaktaḥ asi me sakhā ca','<b>you are my devotee and friend</b>'),('rahasyam hi etat uttamam','<b>this is the supreme secret</b>')],
 'Why does Arjuna receive it? Because he is devotee and friend.',
 'The teaching needs a relationship. Commentators, especially Rāmānuja’s school, stress <i>bhakta</i> and <i>sakhā</i>: love and friendship open the door to the highest teaching. “Secret” does not mean hidden by rule, but rare and precious.',
 'We learn best from those we trust and who care for us. Relationship comes before teaching.',
 'Given to <b>a devotee and friend</b>.'),
X(4,'“HOW COULD YOU HAVE?”',
 S('Arjuna puzzled, comparing the young Krishna with the ancient Sun','A','sun','HOW?'),
 'Arjuna said: Your birth was later; Vivasvān’s birth was earlier. How am I to understand that you taught it in the beginning?',
 [('aparam bhavataḥ janma','<b>your birth is later</b>'),('param janma vivasvataḥ','<b>Vivasvān’s birth earlier</b>'),('katham etat vijānīyām','<b>how should I understand this</b>'),('tvam ādau proktavān iti','<b>that you taught it at the start</b>')],
 'Arjuna raises a logical problem: Kṛṣṇa is his cousin, born recently. How could he have taught the Sun?',
 'Commentators say Arjuna asks so that the world may learn of the avatāra. His question leads to the most famous verses on divine incarnation.',
 'Good questions bring out deep answers. Do not be shy to ask the obvious.',
 'A simple question <b>opens a great answer</b>.'),
X(5,'MANY BIRTHS',
 S('Two trails of past lives, one lit and remembered','K','star','I REMEMBER'),
 'The Lord said: Many births of mine have passed, and of yours, Arjuna. I know them all; you do not know them, scorcher of foes.',
 [('bahūni me vyatītāni','<b>many of mine have passed</b>'),('janmāni tava ca arjuna','<b>births, and of yours</b>'),('tāni aham veda sarvāṇi','<b>I know them all</b>'),('na tvam vettha','<b>you do not know</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa answers: we have both been born many times. The difference is memory.',
 'This affirms rebirth for both. Commentators note the contrast: the soul forgets past lives; the Lord remembers, because his births are not forced by karma but freely chosen.',
 'We are older than this life, says the Gītā. Each life is a chapter, not the whole book.',
 'We have all lived many times; <b>the Lord remembers</b>.'),
X(6,'BY MY OWN POWER',
 S('A blue figure of light descending by choice','K','light','ĀTMA-MĀYĀ'),
 '“Though I am unborn and of imperishable nature, and Lord of all beings, governing my own nature, I come into being by my own power (māyā).”',
 [('ajaḥ api san avyaya-ātmā','<b>though unborn, imperishable</b>'),('bhūtānām īśvaraḥ api san','<b>though Lord of beings</b>'),('prakṛtim svām adhiṣṭhāya','<b>governing my own nature</b>'),('sambhavāmi ātma-māyayā','<b>I come into being by my own power</b>')],
 'How does the Unborn take birth? By his own power, not by karma.',
 '<i>Māyā</i> here is read differently: Śaṅkara as the Lord’s power of appearance; Rāmānuja as his own will and wonderful power, his body being divine and real. All agree the avatāra is free, not bound.',
 'The Divine enters the world by choice, out of care. That is the root of the avatāra idea.',
 'The Unborn is born <b>by his own will</b>.'),
X(7,'“WHENEVER DHARMA DECLINES”',
 S('A dark tide of adharma rising and a light descending','K','storm','YADĀ YADĀ'),
 '“Whenever there is a decline of dharma, Bhārata, and a rise of adharma, then I bring myself forth.”',
 [('yadā yadā hi','<b>whenever</b>'),('dharmasya glāniḥ','<b>a decline of dharma</b>'),('bhavati bhārata','<b>happens, Bhārata</b>'),('abhyutthānam adharmasya','<b>a rise of adharma</b>'),('tadā ātmānam sṛjāmi aham','<b>then I bring myself forth</b>')],
 'The avatāra verse, among the best-known lines in Hinduism.',
 'The Daśāvatāra tradition (see our Daśāvatāra series) grows from this idea. Commentators note <i>yadā yadā</i>, “whenever”: not once, but again and again, as needed.',
 'No darkness is final. Hope is built into the Gītā.',
 'When dharma falls, <b>the Lord comes</b>.'),
X(8,'“AGE AFTER AGE”',
 S('Krishna with four arms protecting the good and driving away a demon','','vishnu','YUGE YUGE'),
 '“To protect the good, to destroy the wicked, and to establish dharma firmly, I come into being age after age.”',
 [('paritrāṇāya sādhūnām','<b>to protect the good</b>'),('vināśāya ca duṣkṛtām','<b>to destroy evil-doers</b>'),('dharma-saṃsthāpana-arthāya','<b>to establish dharma</b>'),('sambhavāmi yuge yuge','<b>I come age after age</b>')],
 'Three purposes of the avatāra: protect, destroy evil, establish dharma.',
 'Commentators stress the first purpose: protection of the good, including their inner protection, comfort and guidance. Destruction of evil serves that. The refrain <i>sambhavāmi yuge yuge</i> is chanted everywhere.',
 'Whatever we do to protect the good and stand for right shares in this divine work.',
 'The Lord comes <b>age after age</b>.'),
X(9,'KNOWING MY BIRTH FREES',
 S('A devotee with a glowing thread to Krishna','KA','light','FREE'),
 '“One who truly knows my divine birth and actions, on leaving the body, is not born again; he comes to me, Arjuna.”',
 [('janma karma ca me divyam','<b>my divine birth and actions</b>'),('evam yaḥ vetti tattvataḥ','<b>who knows thus in truth</b>'),('tyaktvā deham','<b>leaving the body</b>'),('punar-janma na eti','<b>is not born again</b>'),('mām eti','<b>comes to me</b>')],
 'Understanding the avatāra truly is itself liberating.',
 'Why? Commentators say knowing that the Lord takes birth freely, out of love, changes the knower’s heart: it draws him to the Lord and frees him from fear. Bhakti traditions treasure this verse.',
 'Learning the stories of the Divine is not just entertainment; it can transform us.',
 'To know the Lord truly <b>is to be freed</b>.'),
X(10,'MANY HAVE COME',
 S('A line of saints walking toward a light','P','light','MANY HAVE COME'),
 '“Free from passion, fear and anger, absorbed in me, taking refuge in me, purified by the austerity of knowledge, many have attained my being.”',
 [('vīta-rāga-bhaya-krodhāḥ','<b>free of passion, fear, anger</b>'),('man-mayāḥ mām upāśritāḥ','<b>absorbed in me, taking refuge in me</b>'),('bahavaḥ jñāna-tapasā','<b>many, by austerity of knowledge</b>'),('pūtāḥ mat-bhāvam āgatāḥ','<b>purified, attained my being</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa assures Arjuna that many have walked this path before.',
 'The triad passion-fear-anger returns from 2.56. Commentators note the path: freedom from these, absorption, refuge, purification. It is not one person’s secret; “many” have reached it.',
 'You are not alone on the path. Many have walked it, and their lives show it can be done.',
 'Many have walked <b>this path before</b>.'),
X(11,'AS THEY APPROACH ME',
 S('Many coloured paths winding up to one light','','mountain','ALL PATHS'),
 '“As people approach me, so do I receive them. In every way, Pārtha, people follow my path.”',
 [('ye yathā mām prapadyante','<b>as people approach me</b>'),('tān tathā eva bhajāmi aham','<b>so I receive them</b>'),('mama vartma anuvartante','<b>they follow my path</b>'),('manuṣyāḥ pārtha sarvaśaḥ','<b>people everywhere</b>')],
 'God meets each person in the way they come.',
 'Many read this as the Gītā’s broad-mindedness: sincere seekers on many roads arrive at the same goal. Others read it more narrowly: the Lord rewards each according to their approach. Traditions differ on how far it extends.',
 'Respect other people’s sincere paths. The same Light may be seen through many windows.',
 'The Lord meets you <b>as you come</b>.'),
X(12,'QUICK RESULTS',
 S('People offering at shrines of many gods for quick success','','gods','QUICK SUCCESS'),
 '“Desiring success in their actions, people here worship the gods; for in the human world, success born of action comes quickly.”',
 [('kāṅkṣantaḥ karmaṇām siddhim','<b>desiring success of actions</b>'),('yajante iha devatāḥ','<b>worship the gods here</b>'),('kṣipram hi mānuṣe loke','<b>for quickly in the human world</b>'),('siddhiḥ bhavati karmajā','<b>success born of action comes</b>')],
 'Many people worship for quick worldly results, and get them.',
 'This is not condemned, only noted. Commentators say such worship is valid but limited; the higher path aims at freedom, not just success. Chapter 7 (7.20–23) develops the theme.',
 'Praying for success is human. The Gītā invites us to also seek something more lasting.',
 'Worship for results <b>brings results</b>.'),
X(13,'FOUR ORDERS, BY QUALITY AND WORK',
 S('Four figures by type of work, three guna colours behind','','guna','GUṆA · KARMA'),
 '“The fourfold order was created by me according to the division of quality and work. Though I am its maker, know me as the non-doer, the changeless.”',
 [('cātur-varṇyam mayā sṛṣṭam','<b>the fourfold order was created by me</b>'),('guṇa-karma-vibhāgaśaḥ','<b>by division of quality and work</b>'),('tasya kartāram api mām','<b>though me its maker</b>'),('viddhi akartāram avyayam','<b>know me as non-doer, changeless</b>')],
 'A much-debated verse on the four varṇas.',
 'The Gītā says varṇa follows <i>guṇa</i> and <i>karma</i>, quality and work; it does not mention birth here. Many modern teachers read it as a description of temperaments, not a rigid caste system; many traditional commentators tied it to birth. We give both views; the debate continues.',
 'Each person has a nature and gifts. Finding work that fits them is part of a good life.',
 'Varṇa here rests on <b>quality and work</b>; readings differ.'),
X(14,'ACTIONS DO NOT STAIN ME',
 S('A lotus leaf with water drops rolling off','','lotus','UNSTAINED'),
 '“Actions do not stain me, nor do I long for their fruit. One who knows me thus is not bound by actions.”',
 [('na mām karmāṇi limpanti','<b>actions do not stain me</b>'),('na me karma-phale spṛhā','<b>I have no longing for the fruit</b>'),('iti mām yaḥ abhijānāti','<b>who knows me thus</b>'),('karmabhiḥ na saḥ badhyate','<b>is not bound by actions</b>')],
 'The Lord acts without craving, so action leaves no stain.',
 'The model to copy. Commentators say: knowing how the Lord acts teaches us how to act. The lotus-leaf image appears in 5.10.',
 'Act like the lotus leaf: in the water, not wet by it.',
 'Act without craving, and <b>action leaves no stain</b>.'),
X(15,'THE ANCIENTS DID SO',
 S('Ancient seekers working with calm faces','P','sage','DO AS THEY DID'),
 '“Knowing this, the ancient seekers of freedom also acted. So you too should act, as the ancients did of old.”',
 [('evam jñātvā kṛtam karma','<b>knowing thus, action was done</b>'),('pūrvaiḥ api mumukṣubhiḥ','<b>by the ancient seekers of freedom</b>'),('kuru karma eva tasmāt tvam','<b>so you too, act</b>'),('pūrvaiḥ pūrvataram kṛtam','<b>as done by the ancients of old</b>')],
 'Those who sought freedom long ago did not stop acting. Neither should Arjuna.',
 '<i>Mumukṣu</i>, one who longs for freedom. Commentators say: even such seekers worked, because action in the right spirit purifies.',
 'Follow the example of the wise who came before.',
 'The ancients <b>acted, and were free</b>.'),
X(16,'WHAT IS ACTION?',
 S('Krishna holding up a puzzle labelled action','K','mind','KARMA?'),
 '“What is action? What is inaction? Even the wise are confused about this. I will tell you what action is; knowing it, you will be freed from evil.”',
 [('kim karma kim akarma iti','<b>what is action, what is inaction?</b>'),('kavayaḥ api atra mohitāḥ','<b>even sages are confused here</b>'),('tat te karma pravakṣyāmi','<b>I will tell you that action</b>'),('yat jñātvā mokṣyase aśubhāt','<b>knowing which, you will be freed from ill</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa admits the question is subtle: even sages get confused.',
 'The humility is striking. Defining action is not simple, because what looks like action may not bind and what looks like rest may. The next verses unfold the riddle.',
 'Simple-looking questions can be deep. Be patient with them.',
 'Even sages wonder: <b>what is action?</b>'),
X(17,'THE WAY OF ACTION IS DEEP',
 S('A deep well labelled the way of karma','','river','GAHANĀ'),
 '“One must understand what action is, what wrong action is, and what inaction is. Deep is the way of action.”',
 [('karmaṇaḥ hi api boddhavyam','<b>action must be understood</b>'),('boddhavyam ca vikarmaṇaḥ','<b>and wrong action</b>'),('akarmaṇaḥ ca boddhavyam','<b>and inaction</b>'),('gahanā karmaṇaḥ gatiḥ','<b>deep is the way of action</b>')],
 'Three things to understand: right action, wrong action (<i>vikarma</i>), and inaction.',
 '<i>Gahanā karmaṇo gatiḥ</i>, “deep is the way of karma”, has become a proverb in Indian languages. Commentators say each act must be judged by motive, context and knowledge, not by appearance.',
 'Before judging an action, yours or another’s, look deeper. Appearances mislead.',
 '<b>Deep is the way of action</b>.'),
X(18,'ACTION IN INACTION',
 S('A busy worker calm inside, a still sitter racing inside','P','mind','PARADOX'),
 '“One who sees inaction in action and action in inaction is wise among people; he is a yogi, doing all his work.”',
 [('karmaṇi akarma yaḥ paśyet','<b>who sees inaction in action</b>'),('akarmaṇi ca karma yaḥ','<b>and action in inaction</b>'),('saḥ buddhimān manuṣyeṣu','<b>he is wise among people</b>'),('saḥ yuktaḥ kṛtsna-karma-kṛt','<b>a yogi, doing all work</b>')],
 'The Gītā’s riddle.',
 'One reading: a sage acting without ego is really “not doing”; a lazy person idle but craving is really “doing” and binding himself. Śaṅkara reads it as seeing the actionless Self in all activity. Either way, the inside counts.',
 'Check the inside of your actions: where is the ego doing, and where is it at rest?',
 'What counts is <b>the inside of an action</b>.'),
X(19,'BURNT IN THE FIRE OF KNOWLEDGE',
 S('Actions burnt in a fire of knowledge, leaving no seed','','fire','JÑĀNA-AGNI'),
 '“One whose undertakings are free from desire and its schemes, whose actions are burnt in the fire of knowledge: the wise call him a paṇḍita.”',
 [('yasya sarve samārambhāḥ','<b>whose undertakings all</b>'),('kāma-saṅkalpa-varjitāḥ','<b>are free of desire and plans</b>'),('jñāna-agni-dagdha-karmāṇam','<b>actions burnt by knowledge-fire</b>'),('tam āhuḥ paṇḍitam budhāḥ','<b>the wise call him learned</b>')],
 'A wise person’s actions leave no karmic seed, as if roasted in fire.',
 'A roasted seed cannot sprout. Commentators use this image: action burnt by knowledge cannot produce rebirth. <i>Saṅkalpa</i>, the scheming intention behind desire, is also dropped.',
 'Act without inner scheming for reward, and actions become light.',
 'Knowledge <b>roasts the seed of karma</b>.'),
X(20,'DOING, YET NOT DOING',
 S('A content man walking calmly through a busy road','P','light','FREE'),
 '“Giving up attachment to the fruit of action, ever content, depending on nothing, though fully engaged in action, he does nothing at all.”',
 [('tyaktvā karma-phala-āsaṅgam','<b>giving up clinging to results</b>'),('nitya-tṛptaḥ nirāśrayaḥ','<b>ever content, depending on nothing</b>'),('karmaṇi abhipravṛttaḥ api','<b>though fully engaged in action</b>'),('na eva kiñcit karoti saḥ','<b>he does nothing at all</b>')],
 'A free worker: fully active, yet inwardly not “doing” in the binding sense.',
 '<i>Nirāśraya</i>, depending on nothing: not leaning on results or praise for happiness. Commentators say such a person’s work is pure offering.',
 'Work fully and need nothing from it. That is freedom in action.',
 'Fully at work, <b>yet free</b>.'),
X(21,'ONLY THE BODY ACTS',
 S('A body working while the self rests in calm','Y','light','BODY WORKS'),
 '“Without hopes, with mind and self controlled, giving up all possessions, doing only bodily action, he incurs no sin.”',
 [('nirāśīḥ yata-citta-ātmā','<b>without hopes, mind controlled</b>'),('tyakta-sarva-parigrahaḥ','<b>giving up all possessions</b>'),('śārīram kevalam karma','<b>only bodily action</b>'),('kurvan na āpnoti kilbiṣam','<b>doing, incurs no sin</b>')],
 'Such a person acts only to keep the body going, without hoarding.',
 'Śaṅkara reads this verse as describing the renunciant monk, doing only what the body needs. Others apply it to anyone who acts without greed. <i>Parigraha</i>, possessiveness, is the root of much trouble.',
 'Need little, hold little. Simplicity makes action lighter.',
 'Holding nothing, <b>no sin clings</b>.'),
X(22,'CONTENT WITH WHAT COMES',
 S('A person accepting whatever falls into an open hand','P','gift','WHATEVER COMES'),
 '“Content with what comes by itself, beyond the pairs of opposites, free from envy, the same in success and failure: even acting, he is not bound.”',
 [('yadṛcchā-lābha-santuṣṭaḥ','<b>content with what comes unsought</b>'),('dvandva-atītaḥ','<b>beyond opposites</b>'),('vimatsaraḥ','<b>free of envy</b>'),('samaḥ siddhau asiddhau ca','<b>same in success and failure</b>'),('kṛtvā api na nibadhyate','<b>even acting, is not bound</b>')],
 'The marks of a free worker: contentment, evenness, no envy.',
 '<i>Matsara</i>, envy, is one of the six inner enemies in Indian thought. Commentators note this verse gathers the marks of karma yoga from chapter 2 into one line.',
 'Envy steals joy. Contentment with what comes is a quiet wealth.',
 'Content, even, <b>free of envy</b>.'),
X(23,'WORK DISSOLVES',
 S('Work melting away like ice in sunlight','','sun','DISSOLVES'),
 '“For one free of attachment, liberated, whose mind is fixed in knowledge, acting for yajña, all action dissolves away.”',
 [('gata-saṅgasya muktasya','<b>of one free of attachment, liberated</b>'),('jñāna-avasthita-cetasaḥ','<b>mind fixed in knowledge</b>'),('yajñāya ācarataḥ karma','<b>acting for sacrifice</b>'),('samagram pravilīyate','<b>dissolves entirely</b>')],
 'For such a person, karma melts away and leaves no trace.',
 'The link back to 3.9: work done as yajña does not bind. This sets up the next verse, where everything in the act of offering becomes Brahman.',
 'Offer your work, and the burden of it lightens.',
 'Offered work <b>melts away</b>.'),
X(24,'BRAHMĀRPAṆAM',
 S('A sacred fire where everything glows with the same light','P','fire','ALL IS BRAHMAN'),
 '“The offering is Brahman, the oblation is Brahman, poured by Brahman into the fire of Brahman. Brahman will be reached by the one who sees Brahman in every action.”',
 [('brahma arpaṇam','<b>the offering is Brahman</b>'),('brahma haviḥ','<b>the oblation is Brahman</b>'),('brahma-agnau brahmaṇā hutam','<b>poured by Brahman into Brahman’s fire</b>'),('brahma eva tena gantavyam','<b>Brahman is to be reached by him</b>'),('brahma-karma-samādhinā','<b>absorbed in Brahman in action</b>')],
 'Chanted before meals in many homes, turning eating into worship.',
 'Every part of the act, giver, gift, fire, goal, is seen as the Divine. Śaṅkara reads this as the vision of the knower who sees only Brahman; others as a meditation to sanctify every act.',
 'Try this verse before a meal or a task. It changes how the moment feels.',
 'See the Divine <b>in every part of the act</b>.'),
X(25,'OFFERINGS TO THE GODS, OR TO BRAHMAN',
 S('Two fires: one with offerings to gods, one where the self is offered','','fire','DAIVA · BRAHMA'),
 '“Some yogis offer yajña to the gods alone; others offer yajña by means of yajña into the fire of Brahman.”',
 [('daivam eva apare yajñam','<b>some, sacrifice to the gods</b>'),('yoginaḥ paryupāsate','<b>the yogis perform</b>'),('brahma-agnau apare','<b>others, into Brahman’s fire</b>'),('yajñena eva upajuhvati','<b>offer by sacrifice itself</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa begins a list of different kinds of yajña.',
 'Commentators read the second kind as offering the individual self into Brahman: knowledge in which the self merges into the Absolute. The list (4.25–32) shows that yajña is wider than ritual.',
 'There are many ways to offer. Find the ones that fit you.',
 'Many kinds of <b>offering</b>.'),
X(26,'OFFERING THE SENSES',
 S('A yogi with closed eyes and ears, senses offered into a fire of restraint','Y','fire','SAṂYAMA'),
 '“Some offer hearing and the other senses into the fires of restraint; others offer sound and the other objects into the fires of the senses.”',
 [('śrotra-ādīni indriyāṇi','<b>hearing and other senses</b>'),('anye saṃyama-agniṣu','<b>others, into fires of restraint</b>'),('śabda-ādīn viṣayān','<b>sound and other objects</b>'),('indriya-agniṣu juhvati','<b>offer into the fires of the senses</b>')],
 'Two kinds: restraint of the senses, and enjoying objects as an offering.',
 'The first is the ascetic way; the second, the householder’s: using the senses without attachment, as if offering what they receive. Both are valid yajña in the Gītā’s view.',
 'You can offer by restraint, or by enjoying mindfully. Both can be sacred.',
 'Restraint or mindful use: <b>both are offerings</b>.'),
X(27,'THE FIRE OF SELF-CONTROL',
 S('A yogi offering all activity into an inner fire lit by knowledge','Y','light','ĀTMA-SAṂYAMA'),
 '“Others offer all the actions of the senses and of the life-breath into the fire of the yoga of self-control, kindled by knowledge.”',
 [('sarvāṇi indriya-karmāṇi','<b>all actions of the senses</b>'),('prāṇa-karmāṇi ca apare','<b>and of the breath, others</b>'),('ātma-saṃyama-yoga-agnau','<b>into the fire of self-control</b>'),('juhvati jñāna-dīpite','<b>offer, lit by knowledge</b>')],
 'A deeper offering: all activity gathered into self-control, lit by knowledge.',
 'This points toward meditation (chapter 6), where all activity is withdrawn into stillness. Commentators note “lit by knowledge”: control without understanding is dull.',
 'Self-control is an offering when it comes from understanding, not mere force.',
 'Self-control <b>lit by knowledge</b>.'),
X(28,'WEALTH, AUSTERITY, STUDY',
 S('A giver with gifts, an ascetic, a student with a book','P','book','DRAVYA · TAPAS · SVĀDHYĀYA'),
 '“Others offer wealth, austerity, or yoga; and ascetics with firm vows offer study and knowledge.”',
 [('dravya-yajñāḥ','<b>those whose offering is wealth</b>'),('tapo-yajñāḥ','<b>whose offering is austerity</b>'),('yoga-yajñāḥ tathā apare','<b>whose offering is yoga</b>'),('svādhyāya-jñāna-yajñāḥ ca','<b>whose offering is study and knowledge</b>'),('saṃśita-vratāḥ','<b>of firm vows</b>')],
 'More kinds: charity, austerity, practice, study.',
 '<i>Svādhyāya</i>, study of scripture, is itself worship. Commentators say giving money, fasting, practising and studying are all yajñas when offered without ego.',
 'Your giving, your discipline, your study: all can be offerings.',
 'Giving, discipline and study <b>are yajña too</b>.'),
X(29,'OFFERING THE BREATH',
 S('A yogi with breath flowing in and out, held still in the middle','Y','mind','PRĀṆĀYĀMA'),
 '“Others offer the in-breath into the out-breath, and the out-breath into the in-breath; restraining the course of both, they are devoted to breath control.”',
 [('apāne juhvati prāṇam','<b>offer prāṇa into apāna</b>'),('prāṇe apānam tathā apare','<b>and apāna into prāṇa</b>'),('prāṇa-apāna-gatī ruddhvā','<b>restraining both courses</b>'),('prāṇāyāma-parāyaṇāḥ','<b>devoted to breath control</b>')],
 'Breath control as yajña.',
 'This verse is an early reference to prāṇāyāma. Commentators describe the three phases: inhalation, exhalation and retention. The breath links body and mind; calming it calms both.',
 'A few slow breaths are a tiny offering that steadies the whole day.',
 'Breath itself <b>can be an offering</b>.'),
X(30,'OFFERING THE FOOD',
 S('A person eating little, offering the rest','P','food','MODERATE'),
 '“Others, regulating their food, offer life-breaths into life-breaths. All these know yajña, and by yajña their sins are destroyed.”',
 [('apare niyata-āhārāḥ','<b>others, regulating food</b>'),('prāṇān prāṇeṣu juhvati','<b>offer breaths into breaths</b>'),('sarve api ete yajña-vidaḥ','<b>all these know sacrifice</b>'),('yajña-kṣapita-kalmaṣāḥ','<b>their sins destroyed by sacrifice</b>')],
 'Moderate eating as yajña, and a summary: all these are true sacrifices.',
 '<i>Niyata-āhāra</i>, regulated diet, returns in 6.16–17 and 17.8–10. Commentators note the conclusion: many paths, one principle, offering without ego.',
 'Eating moderately is a gift to body and mind alike.',
 'All these <b>are true sacrifices</b>.'),
X(31,'THE NECTAR OF WHAT REMAINS',
 S('A person tasting sweet nectar left after an offering','P','light','AMṚTA'),
 '“Those who eat the nectar of what remains after yajña go to eternal Brahman. Not even this world is for one who offers no yajña; how then the other?”',
 [('yajña-śiṣṭa-amṛta-bhujaḥ','<b>eating the nectar left from sacrifice</b>'),('yānti brahma sanātanam','<b>go to eternal Brahman</b>'),('na ayam lokaḥ asti','<b>not even this world</b>'),('ayajñasya','<b>for one who offers no sacrifice</b>'),('kutaḥ anyaḥ','<b>how then the other?</b>')],
 'Living from what remains after offering leads to Brahman.',
 'This echoes 3.13. Commentators say even worldly happiness depends on give and take; how much more the highest goal.',
 'Give first. Whatever remains will taste sweeter.',
 'What remains after offering <b>is nectar</b>.'),
X(32,'ALL BORN OF ACTION',
 S('Many fires side by side, all lit by action','','fire','MANY YAJÑAS'),
 '“Thus many kinds of yajña are spread out at the mouth of Brahman (the Veda). Know them all as born of action; knowing this, you will be freed.”',
 [('evam bahu-vidhāḥ yajñāḥ','<b>thus many kinds of sacrifice</b>'),('vitatāḥ brahmaṇaḥ mukhe','<b>spread out in the Veda</b>'),('karma-jān viddhi tān sarvān','<b>know them all as born of action</b>'),('evam jñātvā vimokṣyase','<b>knowing thus, you will be freed</b>')],
 'All these yajñas involve action; knowing this frees one.',
 'Śaṅkara reads it as: these all arise from body, speech and mind, not from the Self; knowing the Self as actionless frees. Others: knowing that all action can be offered frees.',
 'Every kind of work can be offered. That is the key.',
 'All yajñas are <b>action offered</b>.'),
X(33,'THE SACRIFICE OF KNOWLEDGE',
 S('A fire with grain and a brighter fire with a book','','book','JÑĀNA-YAJÑA'),
 '“Better than sacrifice of material things is the sacrifice of knowledge, scorcher of foes. All action, Pārtha, finds its completion in knowledge.”',
 [('śreyān dravya-mayāt yajñāt','<b>better than sacrifice of things</b>'),('jñāna-yajñaḥ parantapa','<b>is the sacrifice of knowledge</b>'),('sarvam karma akhilam pārtha','<b>all action in full</b>'),('jñāne parisamāpyate','<b>is completed in knowledge</b>')],
 'The highest offering is understanding.',
 'All action aims at knowing. Commentators agree knowledge is the culmination, though they differ on whether it replaces action (Śaṅkara) or crowns it (Rāmānuja).',
 'Learning and understanding are among the highest offerings we can make.',
 'The best offering is <b>understanding</b>.'),
X(34,'LEARN FROM THOSE WHO SEE',
 S('A student bowing before a teacher under a tree, asking questions','P','sage','ASK · SERVE · BOW'),
 '“Learn it by humble reverence, by questioning, and by service. The wise who have seen the truth will teach you knowledge.”',
 [('tat viddhi praṇipātena','<b>know it by bowing</b>'),('paripraśnena','<b>by asking questions</b>'),('sevayā','<b>by service</b>'),('upadekṣyanti te jñānam','<b>they will teach you knowledge</b>'),('jñāninaḥ tattva-darśinaḥ','<b>the wise who have seen the truth</b>')],
 'How to learn from a teacher: humility, questions, service.',
 'Questions are encouraged: <i>paripraśna</i> means thorough inquiry. The teacher must have <i>seen</i> the truth, not just read it. This verse is the classic description of the guru-disciple relationship.',
 'Find someone who lives what they teach. Approach them humbly, ask honestly, and serve.',
 'Humility, questions, service: <b>the way to learn</b>.'),
X(35,'ALL BEINGS IN YOU, AND IN ME',
 S('A person seeing all beings within himself and within Krishna','Y','family','ALL IN ONE'),
 '“Knowing it, you will not again fall into such delusion, Pāṇḍava; by it you will see all beings in your Self, and then in me.”',
 [('yat jñātvā na punaḥ moham','<b>knowing which, never again delusion</b>'),('evam yāsyasi pāṇḍava','<b>will you fall into</b>'),('yena bhūtāni aśeṣeṇa','<b>by which all beings without exception</b>'),('drakṣyasi ātmani','<b>you will see in your Self</b>'),('atho mayi','<b>and then in me</b>')],
 'The fruit of knowledge: seeing all beings in oneself and in God.',
 'This vision returns in 6.29–30. Commentators read it according to their school: oneness (Śaṅkara) or all beings as the body of God (Rāmānuja). All agree it ends Arjuna’s confusion about “mine” and “theirs”.',
 'When we see others in ourselves, cruelty becomes impossible and kindness natural.',
 'See <b>all beings in the Self</b>.'),
X(36,'THE RAFT OF KNOWLEDGE',
 S('A small raft carrying a man across a stormy sea','P','ocean','JÑĀNA-PLAVA'),
 '“Even if you were the worst of all sinners, you would cross over all evil by the raft of knowledge alone.”',
 [('api cet asi pāpebhyaḥ','<b>even if you are, than sinners</b>'),('sarvebhyaḥ pāpa-kṛttamaḥ','<b>the worst of all</b>'),('sarvam jñāna-plavena eva','<b>by the raft of knowledge alone</b>'),('vṛjinam santariṣyasi','<b>you will cross all evil</b>')],
 'Hope for everyone: no past is too heavy.',
 'A raft carries anyone who climbs on. Commentators note this is not permission to sin, but assurance that the past need not trap us.',
 'No one is beyond help. Begin now.',
 'Knowledge carries even <b>the worst across</b>.'),
X(37,'THE FIRE OF KNOWLEDGE',
 S('A blazing fire turning a woodpile to ash','','fire','TO ASHES'),
 '“As a blazing fire turns firewood to ashes, Arjuna, so the fire of knowledge turns all karma to ashes.”',
 [('yathā edhāṃsi samiddhaḥ','<b>as firewood, a blazing</b>'),('agniḥ bhasmasāt kurute','<b>fire turns to ashes</b>'),('jñāna-agniḥ sarva-karmāṇi','<b>the fire of knowledge, all karma</b>'),('bhasmasāt kurute tathā','<b>turns to ashes likewise</b>')],
 'Knowledge burns karma.',
 'Commentators distinguish kinds of karma: past karma not yet ripened is burnt; karma already bearing fruit (the present body) runs its course, like an arrow already shot. Then the knower is free.',
 'Understanding changes everything. The same past looks different in its light.',
 'Knowledge burns <b>all karma to ash</b>.'),
X(38,'THE PUREST THING',
 S('A clear light flowing into a person','P','light','PAVITRAM'),
 '“Nothing in this world purifies like knowledge. One perfected in yoga finds it in himself in time.”',
 [('na hi jñānena sadṛśam','<b>nothing equals knowledge</b>'),('pavitram iha vidyate','<b>as a purifier here</b>'),('tat svayam yoga-saṃsiddhaḥ','<b>that, one perfected in yoga himself</b>'),('kālena ātmani vindati','<b>finds in himself in time</b>')],
 'Knowledge is the greatest purifier, and it is found within, in time.',
 'Note “in time” and “in himself”: knowledge is not bought; it ripens through yoga. Commentators say practice prepares the ground; knowledge arises by itself.',
 'Be patient. Practice prepares; understanding arrives in its time.',
 'Nothing purifies <b>like knowledge</b>.'),
X(39,'FAITH GAINS KNOWLEDGE',
 S('A person with folded hands, a light pouring into them','P','heart','ŚRADDHĀ'),
 '“The one with faith gains knowledge, devoted to it and master of his senses. Gaining knowledge, he soon reaches supreme peace.”',
 [('śraddhāvān labhate jñānam','<b>the one with faith gains knowledge</b>'),('tat-paraḥ saṃyata-indriyaḥ','<b>devoted, with senses controlled</b>'),('jñānam labdhvā','<b>having gained knowledge</b>'),('parām śāntim acireṇa adhigacchati','<b>soon reaches supreme peace</b>')],
 'Three conditions: faith, devotion, self-control. The result: knowledge and peace.',
 '<i>Śraddhāvān labhate jñānam</i> is a beloved line. Faith here is trust that keeps one practising long enough to see for oneself.',
 'Trust the path enough to walk it. Seeing comes on the way.',
 'The one with faith <b>gains knowledge</b>.'),
X(40,'THE DOUBTER',
 S('A person stuck at a crossroads, unable to choose','P','mind','STUCK'),
 '“The ignorant, faithless, doubting self perishes. For the doubter there is neither this world nor the next, nor happiness.”',
 [('ajñaḥ ca aśraddadhānaḥ ca','<b>the ignorant and faithless</b>'),('saṃśaya-ātmā vinaśyati','<b>the doubter perishes</b>'),('na ayam lokaḥ asti','<b>not this world</b>'),('na paraḥ na sukham','<b>nor the next, nor happiness</b>'),('saṃśaya-ātmanaḥ','<b>for the doubter</b>')],
 'Chronic doubt destroys.',
 'This is not against questioning, which the Gītā welcomes (4.34). It is against the paralysed mind that never commits to anything, and so gains nothing in this world or the next.',
 'Question, then decide. Endless doubt is a trap.',
 'Endless doubt <b>destroys</b>.'),
X(41,'NO BONDS FOR THE ONE WHO KNOWS',
 S('A free person whose doubts are cut, chains broken','Y','chain','FREE'),
 '“Actions do not bind one who has renounced action through yoga, whose doubts are cut by knowledge, and who is master of himself, Dhanañjaya.”',
 [('yoga-sannyasta-karmāṇam','<b>who has renounced action by yoga</b>'),('jñāna-sañchinna-saṃśayam','<b>doubts cut by knowledge</b>'),('ātmavantam','<b>self-possessed</b>'),('na karmāṇi nibadhnanti','<b>actions do not bind</b>')],
 'Yoga, knowledge and self-mastery together free one from action’s bonds.',
 'A summary of the chapter: action renounced through yoga (offered), doubt cut by knowledge, and self-possession. Commentators see karma yoga and jñāna yoga joining here.',
 'Offer the work, clear the doubt, steady the self. Then work is light.',
 'Yoga and knowledge <b>free from bondage</b>.'),
X(42,'CUT DOUBT, STAND UP',
 S('Arjuna raising a sword of light to cut a tangled knot','A','sword','UTTIṢṬHA!'),
 '“So, with the sword of knowledge, cut this doubt in your heart, born of ignorance. Take to yoga, and stand up, Bhārata!”',
 [('tasmāt ajñāna-sambhūtam','<b>therefore, born of ignorance</b>'),('hṛt-stham','<b>dwelling in the heart</b>'),('jñāna-asinā ātmanaḥ','<b>with the sword of knowledge, your own</b>'),('chittvā enam saṃśayam','<b>cutting this doubt</b>'),('yogam ātiṣṭha uttiṣṭha','<b>take to yoga, stand up</b>')],
 'The chapter ends with a sword and a command.',
 'The sword cuts doubt, not people. The final word, <i>uttiṣṭha</i>, “stand up”, echoes 2.3. Commentators note the pattern: every chapter of teaching returns to the call to act.',
 'Cut through doubt and act. Understanding is meant to be lived.',
 'Cut doubt with knowledge, <b>and stand up</b>.'),
]
cover=('<svg width="380" height="268" viewBox="0 0 380 268" role="img" aria-label="Krishna radiant against a dark sky, a rising sun behind him, fires of offering on either side">'
 '<rect width="380" height="268" fill="#0b2a4a" rx="8"/>'+SUN(190,90,44)+GLOW(190,90,90,'#ff8c1a',.25)+'<path d="M0 220 h380 V268 H0z" fill="#c9a26b" stroke="#111"/>'+KR(190,170,1.6,arms4=True)+FIRE(70,236,1.2)+FIRE(310,236,1.2)+'</svg>')
P=dict(ch=4,nv=42,V=V,per=[14,14,14],out='/home/claude/work/gita/Gita_Chapter04.html',
 title='Knowledge and Action · Bhagavad Gītā Chapter 4 · srini.presents',
 desc='Bhagavad Gītā chapter 4, Jñāna Karma Sannyāsa Yoga, every śloka explained: the ancient lineage of the Gītā, “yadā yadā hi dharmasya”, action in inaction, brahmārpaṇam, many kinds of yajña, and the fire of knowledge. All 42 ślokas in IAST, each on two pages.',
 burst='CHAPTER 4 OF 18',h1='KNOWLEDGE AND ACTION',series='BHAGAVAD GĪTĀ · CHAPTER 4 · JÑĀNA KARMA SANNYĀSA YOGA',tag='Yadā yadā hi dharmasya · every śloka explained',cover=cover,
 where=[f'In {chl(3)} Kṛṣṇa taught karma yoga: act, as an offering, for the world, and slay desire.',
  'Chapter 4 reveals who is teaching. Kṛṣṇa says he gave this yoga to the Sun long ago, and that he takes birth age after age to restore dharma. Then he shows how action and knowledge join: in the right spirit every act is a sacrifice, and knowledge burns karma to ash.'],
 wtalk=("Is this where 'yadā yadā hi dharmasya' comes from?","Yes, 4.7–8. After 2.47, probably the most quoted lines of the Gītā."),
 fullnote=FULLNOTE,
 secs=[('THE ANCIENT LINEAGE',1),('THE AVATĀRA',4),('HOW THE LORD WORKS',11),('ACTION AND INACTION',16),('MANY KINDS OF YAJÑA',24),('KNOWLEDGE',33)],
 end=['<b>The lineage (4.1–5).</b> Kṛṣṇa taught this yoga to the Sun; it passed down and was lost; he gives it again to Arjuna.',
  '<b>The avatāra (4.6–10).</b> The Unborn comes age after age to protect the good and restore dharma.',
  '<b>How he works (4.11–15).</b> He meets people as they come; he made the order of guṇa and karma; action does not stain him.',
  '<b>Action and inaction (4.16–32).</b> See action in inaction; offer everything as Brahman; many disciplines are yajña.',
  '<b>Knowledge (4.33–42).</b> Learn from seers by humility, questions and service; knowledge burns karma; cut doubt and stand up.'],
 endsub='Chapter 4 in five lines',
 next=("What's next?",f'{chl(5)}: Renouncing and doing. Arjuna asks again: which is better, giving up action or doing it? Kṛṣṇa says both lead to freedom, but doing, without ego, is easier.'),
 endkey='<b>the Lord comes age after age; knowledge burns karma</b>.',
 foot=FOOT(4,'The reading of 4.13 is genuinely contested; we give the main views without taking sides. '))
build2(P)
